      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs26 #5): LO could be a plain
      ⍝ function to the left of ⍤ with a literal y (F⍤y), but not a
      ⍝ derived function built from a monadic operator (F M⍤y, e.g.
      ⍝ +/⍤1) without manually parenthesizing it first.
      ⍝ Parser::fix_RANK_syntax()'s pos_LO = pos_RANK - 1 grabbed only
      ⍝ the trailing operator (/), not the whole derived function +/,
      ⍝ so +/⍤1 B parenthesized itself as +(/⍤1) B instead of (+/)⍤1 B
      ⍝ -- reported as SYNTAX ERROR.
      ⍝
      ASSERT ( +/⍤1⊢2 3⍴⍳6 )   ≡ ( (+/)⍤1⊢2 3⍴⍳6 )            ⍝ F M ⍤ y
      ASSERT ( +/⍤1⊢2 3⍴⍳6 )   ≡ 6 15
      ASSERT ( +\⍤1⊢2 3⍴⍳6 )   ≡ ( (+\)⍤1⊢2 3⍴⍳6 )            ⍝ F M ⍤ y
      ASSERT ( +⌿⍤1⊢2 3⍴⍳6 )   ≡ ( (+⌿)⍤1⊢2 3⍴⍳6 )            ⍝ F M ⍤ y
      ASSERT ( {⍵}¨⍤1⊢2 3⍴⍳6 ) ≡ ( ({⍵}¨)⍤1⊢2 3⍴⍳6 )          ⍝ F M ⍤ y

      ⍝ regression (found while investigating Blake McBride's Bugs26
      ⍝ #5, same "one token back" bug class but in a different place,
      ⍝ this time with two separate bugs stacked on top of each other):
      ⍝ (+/)[1]M silently ignored the [1] and always reduced the last
      ⍝ axis instead (same wrong result as (+/)[2]M or plain (+/)M).
      ⍝
      ⍝ Bug 1 (Parser.cc): optimize_literal_axes() decided function-axis
      ⍝ vs. value-index via tos[src-1].is_function(), which only looks
      ⍝ at the single token left of [ -- for (+/)[1] that is ), not a
      ⍝ function-typed token, so it silently fell through to the value-
      ⍝ index path (indexing a function value is meaningless, so the
      ⍝ axis got dropped instead of erroring). Fixed with a small local
      ⍝ ends_in_function() helper that peels through a chain of closing
      ⍝ parens, e.g. seeing the / inside (+/). Parser::check_if_value()
      ⍝ did something similar for the unrelated /⌿\⍀ decision, and was
      ⍝ tried first, but was NOT reusable here: its TC_SYMBOL case
      ⍝ assumed symbols already carry their resolved tag (TOK_P_SYMB
      ⍝ etc.), true only once Prefix.cc runs -- reusing it this early
      ⍝ misread a plain not-yet-tagged variable, e.g. A in Z←A[3], as a
      ⍝ function instead of a value, breaking ordinary value indexing
      ⍝ (caught by Bracket_Index.tc via the full suite, not this file).
      ⍝
      ⍝ Bug 2 (DerivedFunction.cc), only exposed once bug 1 was fixed:
      ⍝ (+/) itself reduces to an axis-less Derived_LO_M (Prefix.cc
      ⍝ reduce_F_M__()), and the trailing [1] then reaches it only as
      ⍝ the X parameter of eval_XB() -- which checked its own (always
      ⍝ unset, for this class) member "axis" instead of the X it was
      ⍝ actually given, silently discarding X either way. Same latent
      ⍝ bug existed in Derived_LO_M_X::eval_XB(), fixed identically
      ⍝ though not known to be reachable there. +/[1]M never hit any of
      ⍝ this: adjacency lets reduce_F_M_C_() build an axis-aware
      ⍝ Derived_LO_M_X up front instead.
      ⍝
      ASSERT ( +/[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )   ≡ ( (+/)[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )              ⍝ (F M)[y]
      ASSERT ( +/[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )   ≡ 5 7 9
      ASSERT ( +/[2]2 3⍴⍳6 )   ≡ ( (+/)[2]2 3⍴⍳6 )              ⍝ (F M)[y]
      ASSERT ( +\[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )   ≡ ( (+\)[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )              ⍝ (F M)[y]
      ASSERT ( +⌿[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )   ≡ ( (+⌿)[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )              ⍝ (F M)[y]

      ⍝ regression (found investigating Blake McBride's Bugs26 #5, same
      ⍝ area, unrelated root cause): (⌽[1])B, a parenthesized function
      ⍝ bound to its own literal axis, was a SYNTAX ERROR even for this
      ⍝ plain call with no operator at all involved -- "F C" (function,
      ⍝ axis) alone, with nothing after C to complete some other, longer
      ⍝ phrase (e.g. F C M), never reduced to anything by itself, so the
      ⍝ shift-reduce engine ran off the end of the statement with the
      ⍝ closing ) still unable to match any phrase. Fixed by adding a
      ⍝ new "LPAR F C RPAR" grammar phrase (tools/phrase_gen.def, Bif
      ⍝ Prefix.cc reduce_LPAR_F_C_RPAR()) that collapses it to a single
      ⍝ derived function, the same way the existing "LPAR F RPAR" phrase
      ⍝ already does for a plain (F).
      ⍝
      ⍝ That fix then exposed a second, previously-dormant bug: once
      ⍝ (⌽[1]) could reduce, (⌽[1])2 3⍴⍳6 mis-bound as (⌽[1])2 3⍴⍳6,
      ⍝ i.e. (⌽[1]) applied only to "2 3" (reversing it to "3 2") before
      ⍝ the dyadic ⍴ ever got its real arguments, giving 3 2⍴⍳6 instead
      ⍝ of (⌽[1])(2 3⍴⍳6). Root cause: Prefix::is_value_parenthesis(),
      ⍝ used to decide whether an upcoming (...) is a value or a
      ⍝ function (to resolve exactly this kind of shift/reduce
      ⍝ priority conflict), already had one fix for a parenthesized
      ⍝ function-with-axis ("was missing: landed on '[' itself instead
      ⍝ of past it, misclassifying e.g. (⌽[X]) as a value instead of a
      ⍝ function") -- but that fix only recognized the raw, unoptimized
      ⍝ [ ... ] token triple. Parser::optimize_literal_axes() (a
      ⍝ Parser.cc pass that runs before Prefix.cc ever sees the tokens)
      ⍝ already collapses a literal [1] into a single TOK_FAXIS token,
      ⍝ so that fix's TC_R_BRACK check never matched, next stayed
      ⍝ TC_INDEX, and is_value_parenthesis() fell through to its
      ⍝ default "return true" (value), causing the wrong shift/reduce
      ⍝ decision. Latent since TOK_FAXIS was introduced; invisible until
      ⍝ now because (⌽[1]) itself was always a SYNTAX ERROR before this
      ⍝ session's grammar-phrase fix made it reducible at all.
      ⍝
      ASSERT ( ⌽[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )    ≡ ( (⌽[1])2 3⍴⍳6 )                ⍝ (F C) B
      ASSERT ( ⌽[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )    ≡ 2 3⍴4 5 6 1 2 3
      ASSERT ( ,[1]2 3⍴⍳6 )    ≡ ( (,[1])2 3⍴⍳6 )                ⍝ (F C) B

      ⍝ regression: same as the literal-N assertions just above, but
      ⍝ with a symbolic (variable) axis instead of a literal one. All
      ⍝ the literal-N assertions above only exercise the OPTIONAL
      ⍝ Parser::optimize_literal_axes() path (DO_FT_LITERAL_AXIS, a
      ⍝ compile-time-tunable optimization that only fires for a literal
      ⍝ N, and could be disabled entirely) -- a variable axis bypasses
      ⍝ that pass completely, since Parser.cc cannot resolve a symbol's
      ⍝ value at parse time, so the raw [ ... ] tokens reach Prefix.cc
      ⍝ unresolved and go through a wholly different, always-present
      ⍝ path: Prefix::is_value_parenthesis()'s pre-existing TC_R_BRACK
      ⍝ case (unaffected by this session's TOK_FAXIS fix above, and
      ⍝ already correct before this session), DerivedFunction.cc's
      ⍝ eval_XB() fix, and the new LPAR F C RPAR grammar phrase, all
      ⍝ still needed even here since (F)/(F C) still has to reduce and
      ⍝ still has to correctly thread the [N]/axis through. Confirmed
      ⍝ this symbolic path is independently correct, not just the
      ⍝ literal one -- and confirmed via full reverts that it is the
      ⍝ eval_XB() and grammar-phrase fixes doing the real work here,
      ⍝ not the (here: inapplicable) optimize_literal_axes()/
      ⍝ is_value_parenthesis() TOK_FAXIS fixes.
      ⍝
      N ← 1 ◊ ASSERT ( ⌽[N]2 3⍴⍳6 )  ≡ ( (⌽[N])2 3⍴⍳6 )            ⍝ (F C) B, symbolic C
      N ← 1 ◊ ASSERT ( +/[N]2 3⍴⍳6 ) ≡ ( (+/)[N]2 3⍴⍳6 )           ⍝ (F M)[y], symbolic y
      N ← 2 ◊ ASSERT ( +/[N]2 3⍴⍳6 ) ≡ ( (+/)[N]2 3⍴⍳6 )           ⍝ (F M)[y], symbolic y

      ⍝ Bugs28 #57 regression (Bugs27 #56 residual): f⍤y (like ¨,
      ⍝ ∘.f, and f.g) still broke when the operand changed ⎕IO --
      ⍝ Z__LO_RANK_X5_B (monadic) and Z__A_LO_RANK_X7_B (dyadic),
      ⍝ Macro.def, indexed μ1/μ2/μ6 with the raw loop counter, seeded
      ⍝ from ⎕IO ONCE at the start; if LO's own ⎕IO←0 (global, not
      ⍝ auto-localized in a lambda) persisted past its first call,
      ⍝ every later cell's indexing silently misaligned instead of
      ⍝ INDEX/RANK-ERRORing outright. Fixed the same self-correcting
      ⍝ way as the already-fixed monadic ¨ (Z__LO_EACH_B): recompute
      ⍝ ⎕IO+(N-savedIO) fresh at each use instead of trusting N alone.
      ⍝
